================================================================================
5. FINDINGS (for the author; none blocking)
================================================================================

N1 (low, security). copy_from_user/copy_to_user check the address is in the user
  half and translate through the caller's CR3, but they never test the PTE's
  user/supervisor bit. Today that is safe because create_process_pml4 zeroes
  PML4[0..256] and map_user_page is the only writer of the low half, so every
  low-half page is a user page. It becomes a hole the moment anything maps a
  supervisor page below 0x0000_8000_0000_0000. A U-bit check in translate (or in
  the copy helpers) would make the invariant local instead of global.

N2 (medium, test). The K6-1 isolation check measures `secret_intact` before the
  snooper runs (see section 3). The claim it makes, "A's memory intact" after
  the attempt, is not what is measured. Move the read after run_user_process, or
  check both before and after and compare.

N3 (low, API). sys_brk returns the current break on failure instead of an error
  code, so a caller cannot distinguish "grew to exactly what you asked" from
  "refused". Linux brk returns the *new* break if it succeeded and the old break
  otherwise; callers compare. If that is the intended contract, say it in the
  doc comment; the test only asserts success.

N4 (low, mmap). `prot` is decoded only for write (`prot & PROT_WRITE` or prot==0
  means writable). PROT_NONE is therefore not honoured: mmap(addr, len,
  PROT_NONE) returns a writable page. PROT_EXEC is not distinguished either
  because the kernel's user mappings carry no NX. That is a real limitation of
  the memory syscalls as shipped, not a test gap, and it should be listed in
  docs/k6-design.md's remaining work rather than left implicit.

N5 (informational). The fd table is per process and fd 0-2 are left unused
  (allocation starts at 3), which is right for K6-3's write/exit work. Nothing
  currently refuses open() for a process that already holds 13 open files: it
  returns ERR_INVAL, which is a confusing errno for "too many open files"
  (EMFILE would be -24). Cosmetic.

